$DIR/test.p:3:15: map element type F is an owner, which a map cannot hold: reading the map copies its elements
    An owner owns memory or another resource and releases it when it is dropped,
    so it moves instead of being copied. A map copies its keys and elements as
    Go's does: v := m[k] and range give copies, and m2 := m shares the table.
    Two copies of an owner would both release what it owns, so a map cannot hold
    one.
    Go's garbage collector frees what a map's values refer to. Plutonium frees
    memory when its owner is dropped, so a map, whose elements are copied
    freely, can hold values and counted references but not owners.
    Store a counted reference to the value instead, which copies by counting and
    releases the value with its last copy:
        m := map[string]synced[F]{}
    (see "Map types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.10)
    (run "pluto explain E3213" for a longer explanation)
